{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_079","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"asfRequireDest lsfRequireDestTag","location_hint":"paragraph 302","char_start":12307,"char_end":12339,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_080","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"To enable the asfDisableMaster or asfNoFreeze flags, you must authorize the transaction by signing it with the master key pair. You cannot use a regular key pair or a multi-signature. You can disable asfDisableMaster (that is, re-enable the master key pair) using a regular key pair or multi-signature.","location_hint":"paragraph 303","char_start":12343,"char_end":12645,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_081","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"The following Transaction flags ( tf flags), specific to the AccountSet transaction type, serve the same purpose. Due to limited space, some settings do not have associated tf flags, and new tf flags are not being added to the AccountSet transaction type. You can use a combination of tf and asf flags to enable multiple settings with a single transaction.","location_hint":"paragraph 304","char_start":12647,"char_end":13003,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_082","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"Flag Name Hex Value Decimal Value Replaced by AccountSet Flag","location_hint":"paragraph 305","char_start":13007,"char_end":13068,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_083","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"tfRequireDestTag 0x00010000 65536 asfRequireDest ( SetFlag )","location_hint":"paragraph 306","char_start":13072,"char_end":13132,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_084","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"tfOptionalDestTag 0x00020000 131072 asfRequireDest ( ClearFlag )","location_hint":"paragraph 307","char_start":13134,"char_end":13198,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_085","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"tfRequireAuth 0x00040000 262144 asfRequireAuth ( SetFlag )","location_hint":"paragraph 308","char_start":13200,"char_end":13258,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_086","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"tfOptionalAuth 0x00080000 524288 asfRequireAuth ( ClearFlag )","location_hint":"paragraph 309","char_start":13260,"char_end":13321,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_087","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"tfDisallowXRP 0x00100000 1048576 asfDisallowXRP ( SetFlag )","location_hint":"paragraph 310","char_start":13323,"char_end":13382,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_088","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"tfAllowXRP 0x00200000 2097152 asfDisallowXRP ( ClearFlag )","location_hint":"paragraph 311","char_start":13384,"char_end":13442,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_089","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"The numeric values of tf and asf flags in transactions do not match up with the values they set in the accounts \"at rest\" in the ledger. To read the flags of an account in the ledger, see AccountRoot flags .","location_hint":"paragraph 313","char_start":13457,"char_end":13664,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_090","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"Blocking Incoming Transactions","location_hint":"paragraph 314","char_start":13666,"char_end":13696,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_091","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"Incoming transactions with unclear purposes may be an inconvenience for financial institutions, who would have to recognize when a customer made a mistake, and then potentially refund accounts or adjust balances depending on the mistake. The asfRequireDest and asfDisallowXRP flags are intended to protect users from accidentally sending funds in a way that is unclear about the reason the funds were sent.","location_hint":"paragraph 315","char_start":13698,"char_end":14104,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_092","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"For example, a destination tag is typically used to identify which hosted balance should be credited when a financial institution receives a payment. If the destination tag is omitted, it may be unclear which account should be credited, creating a need for refunds, among other problems. By using the asfRequireDest tag, you can ensure that every incoming payment has a destination tag, which makes it harder for others to send you an ambiguous payment by accident.","location_hint":"paragraph 316","char_start":14106,"char_end":14571,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_093","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"You can protect against unwanted incoming payments for non-XRP currencies by not creating trust lines in those currencies. Since XRP does not require trust, the asfDisallowXRP flag is used to discourage users from sending XRP to an account. However, this flag is not enforced in the XRP Ledger protocol because it could potentially cause accounts to become unusable if they run out of XRP. Instead, client applications should disallow or discourage XRP payments to accounts with the asfDisallowXRP flag enabled.","location_hint":"paragraph 317","char_start":14573,"char_end":15084,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_094","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"If you want to block all incoming payments, you can enable Deposit Authorization . This prevents any transaction from sending money to you, even XRP, unless your account is below the reserve requirement .","location_hint":"paragraph 318","char_start":15086,"char_end":15290,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_095","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"If the DisallowIncoming amendment is enabled, you also have the option to block all incoming Checks, NFTokenOffers, Payment Channels, and trust lines. It is generally harmless to be on the receiving end of these objects, but they can block you from deleting your account and it can be confusing to have objects you didn&#x27;t expect mixed in with the list of objects you created. To block incoming objects, use one or more of these account flags:","location_hint":"paragraph 319","char_start":15292,"char_end":15739,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_096","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"asfDisallowIncomingCheck - for Check objects","location_hint":"paragraph 320","char_start":15743,"char_end":15787,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_097","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"asfDisallowIncomingNFTOffer - for NFTokenOffer objects","location_hint":"paragraph 321","char_start":15789,"char_end":15843,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_098","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"asfDisallowIncomingPayChan - for PayChannel objects","location_hint":"paragraph 322","char_start":15845,"char_end":15896,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_099","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"asfDisallowIncomingTrustline - for RippleState (trust line) objects","location_hint":"paragraph 323","char_start":15898,"char_end":15965,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_100","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"When a transaction would create one of these ledger entries, if the destination account has the corresponding flag enabled, the transaction fails with the result code tecNO_PERMISSION . Unlike Deposit Authorization, these settings do not prevent you from receiving payments in general. Also, enabling this setting doesn&#x27;t stop you from creating these types of objects yourself (unless the destination of your transaction is also using the setting, of course).","location_hint":"paragraph 324","char_start":15969,"char_end":16433,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_101","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"The TransferRate field specifies a fee to charge whenever counterparties transfer the currency you issue.","location_hint":"paragraph 326","char_start":16449,"char_end":16554,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
{"excerpt_id":"ex_097142ab08d3_102","source_id":"src_097142ab08d3","source_hash":"7451d4268f8c681081bc1fd8c4ca0b13d203a57018b7e284d69de39fd7df94fb","text":"In the HTTP and WebSocket APIs, the transfer fee is represented as an integer, the amount that must be sent for 1 billion units to arrive. For example, a 20% transfer fee is represented as the value 1200000000 . The value cannot be less than 1000000000. (Less than that would indicate giving away money for sending transactions, which is exploitable.) You can specify 0 as a shortcut for 1000000000 , meaning no fee.","location_hint":"paragraph 327","char_start":16556,"char_end":16972,"origin":"raw_text","created_at":"2026-05-09T18:54:30.083Z"}
